            <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">Building a Symptom Reference Tool with a Medical API</h1>
            <p class="lead">A common request from users of any health app: "what does this symptom mean?" This walks through building a lookup tool that answers that &mdash; general information about a symptom, not an assessment of what's wrong with the person asking.</p>

            <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
                <strong>Important:</strong> this is a <strong>reference lookup tool</strong>, not a diagnostic symptom checker. It looks up and explains a symptom by name. It does not take a user's reported symptoms and suggest what condition they have &mdash; that's a clinical function Meducate's <a href="/terms" class="alert-link">terms of service</a> explicitly don't permit. See our <a href="/blog/what-is-a-medical-data-api" class="alert-link">medical data API guide</a> for the full line between the two.
            </div>

            <hr>

            <h2 class="fw-semibold">What we're building</h2>
            <p>A page where a user types a symptom &mdash; "chest pain," "shortness of breath," "fatigue" &mdash; and gets back a plain-language description: what the symptom is, what commonly causes it, and what general self-care or next steps are typically associated with it. That's it. No "here's what you probably have," no ranked list of likely diagnoses. Just an explanation of the symptom itself.</p>

            <hr>

            <h2 class="fw-semibold">Step 1: search for the symptom</h2>
            <p>Meducate's <code>/topics/search</code> endpoint takes a query and an optional <code>type</code> filter. Symptoms are their own topic type, so filtering to it keeps results relevant when a search term could match multiple kinds of topics.</p>

            <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>curl -H "X-Api-Key: YOUR_API_KEY" \
  "https://api.meducateapi.com/api/v1/topics/search?query=chest+pain&amp;type=Symptom"</code></pre>

            <p>The response is a paginated list of matching topics with a name, a short summary, and the category it belongs to. Enough to render a search results list or a set of suggestions as the user types.</p>

            <hr>

            <h2 class="fw-semibold">Step 2: fetch the full topic</h2>
            <p>Once the user picks a result, fetch the full topic by name for the structured detail view:</p>

            <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>curl -H "X-Api-Key: YOUR_API_KEY" \
  "https://api.meducateapi.com/api/v1/topics/Chest%20Pain"</code></pre>

            <p>A symptom topic's <code>observations</code>, <code>factors</code>, and <code>actions</code> fields map cleanly onto a reference page: how the symptom typically presents, what's commonly associated with or causes it, and general management or when-to-seek-care guidance. Render those three lists under their own headings and you have a page that reads like a proper reference entry, not a wall of API JSON.</p>

            <hr>

            <h2 class="fw-semibold">Step 3: keep it a lookup, not an assessment</h2>
            <p>The design choice that keeps this tool on the right side of the line: it only ever operates on <em>one symptom the user explicitly picked</em>. It doesn't collect multiple symptoms, weigh them against each other, or output "you might have X." A few things that would cross that line, worth avoiding on purpose:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Letting a user select several symptoms and returning a ranked list of possible conditions</li>
                <li>Any UI copy implying the tool is assessing <em>them</em>, rather than explaining a term they looked up</li>
                <li>Surfacing a "likelihood" or "match score" against specific diagnoses</li>
            </ul>
            <p>A symptom glossary that happens to be searchable is a genuinely useful, low-risk thing to build. A symptom checker that infers diagnoses is a different, much heavier undertaking that needs clinical validation Meducate isn't built to provide.</p>

            <hr>

